   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to providing\nfor alternative penalties for a first time violation of certain provisions of\nthe alcoholic beverage control law; and to repeal certain provisions of such\nlaw related thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "S5979",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5979-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §130, rpld §130 sub 5, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5979\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nproviding for alternative penalties for a first time violation of\ncertain provisions of the alcoholic beverage control law; and to repeal\ncertain provisions of such law related thereto\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo provide the State Liquor Authority (SLA) with the authority to\nprovide a cure period or other opportunity for ameliorative action to\nfor a first-time violation of the Alcoholic Beverage Control Law (ABCL),\nan authority rule, or regulation, if such violation is related to admin-\nistrative or paperwork violations that are not a health or safety risk.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill amends the ABCL to provide SLA with the ability to provide a\ncure period or other opportunity for ameliorative action for a first-\ntime violation of the Alcoholic Beverage Control Law (ABCL), or an\nauthority rule or regulation, if such violation is related to adminis-\ntrative or paperwork issues. These first-time violations would need to\nbe determined by the SLA to be de minimus in nature, and would not\nendanger health, safety, environment, or other protections provided in\nlaw. Such cure period would provide SLA licensed individuals the oppor-\ntunity to take ameliorative action to correct the violation and upon the\nsuccessful correction would prevent the imposition of penalties on the\nparty or parties subject to enforcement of such violation. Finally, this\nbill would repeal a duplicative section of law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2019, the Legislature passed legislation that provides a similar cure\nperiod for small businesses (Chapter 731, L. 2019); however, this meas-\nure does not apply to SLA enforcement of the ABCL. This bill would clar-\nify that SLA has the authority to provide a cure period or other oppor-\ntunity for ameliorative action to for a first-time violation of the\nABCL.\n\nThis bill is even more necessary today as small businesses, many of\nwhich carry licenses issued by the SLA, struggle to survive and stay\nopen because of the COVID-19 pandemic response. SLA is not currently\nauthorized to provide the flexibility to provide a cure period for what\namounts to paperwork and other administrative first-time violations by\nlicensees. As businesses begin to re-open and reengage their customers,\nmany will have unintentionally missed a filing deadline or report. So\nlong as health, safety and environment are protected, the SLA should\nhave and be encouraged to use flexibility when determining if fines or\nother penalties are imposed.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew 2021.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on 45-day after enacted.\n",
